  <p class="lede first reveal">The store is live. The product is in the supplier app. And then nothing happens, because a Shopify store on day one is a shop on a street nobody has walked down yet.</p>
  <p class="reveal">This is where most new stores stall: not at the build, but at the gap between "it exists" and "a stranger paid for something." Most advice for closing that gap is <em>run ads</em> or <em>grow a following</em>, and if you're building this around a day job without showing your face, neither is on the table yet.</p>
  <p class="reveal">So this is the third route. You go where your buyer already asks questions, you make one product page that answers hers, you collect twenty email addresses, and you let the product be the face. The build order is the same as everywhere in this house: product first, store second, fulfilment app third, buyers last. This is the last step.</p>

  <ShopifyCTA heading="No store yet? Start here." />

  <div class="chapter  reveal"><span class="bignum">01</span><div class="num">Chapter One</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="why-ten">Why ten, and why strangers</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">Ten sales to strangers prove three things at once: the product is wanted, the page converts, and the price is right. Ten sales to people who love you prove that people love you.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Friends-and-family orders feel like progress and they aren't. Your sister buys the mug because she's your sister. She'd buy it at twice the price with a worse photo. Her order tells you nothing about whether a woman in another city, who has never heard your name, will look at the same page and reach for her card.</p>
  <p class="reveal">A stranger's order is different. She arrived from somewhere, so one channel works. She read the page and didn't leave, so the page holds. She saw the shipping cost and paid anyway, so the price survived the checkout. One stranger could be a fluke. Ten is a pattern. It isn't a business yet, but it's a test that came back positive, and it tells you which thing to do more of.</p>
  <div class="box tip reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✦</span> The Edit</span><p>Keep two counts from day one: sales, and sales to people you don't know. Only the second count decides anything.</p></div>
  <p class="deeper reveal"><span>Go deeper —</span> <a href="/learn/shopify-store-guide">How to Start a Shopify Store →</a></p>

  <ArticleImage src="/images/library/detail-hands-writing-journal.jpg" alt="Hands writing in a journal \u2014 The Digital Income Edit" caption="The first ten come from being useful" />

  <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">02</span><div class="num">Chapter Two</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="asking">Go where people are already asking</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">Your first buyers are already in a Facebook group or a forum, asking the exact question your product answers. Go there, answer it properly, and let the link come second.</p>
  <p class="reveal">You don't have an audience. Somebody else does. For almost every product a beginner would sell, there is a group where her person already lives: nurses on nights, first-time dog owners, teachers setting up a classroom. Every day in those groups, someone asks a question your product is the answer to.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Find three groups, not thirty.</strong> Pick the ones where members actually reply to each other. Two hundred people who talk beats fifty thousand who post and leave.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Answer the actual question.</strong> If she asks what to put in a gift basket for a new grad nurse, give her a real list. Be useful first, without holding anything back for the link. People can feel a sales pitch wearing a helpful hat, and admins feel it faster.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Link only when asked, or when the rules allow it.</strong> Most groups have a promo day, a pinned thread, or a rule that says "share your shop if someone asks." Read the rules first. Breaking them costs you the group, and the group is the whole point.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Keep a short, honest bio.</strong> One line that says what you make and who it's for. When someone clicks your name after a useful answer, that line does the selling you're not allowed to do in the thread. It's also how you handle the inevitable "what store do you use?" question: name the platform, say what you sell, leave the numbers out. They don't need to know how it's going. They need to know it's real.</p>
  <div class="box check reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✓</span> The group rule</span><p>Three groups · Real answers · Link only when invited · One-line bio. Two weeks of this and you'll know exactly which question your product answers best, because you'll have typed it twenty times.</p></div>
  <p class="deeper reveal"><span>Go deeper —</span> <a href="/learn/what-to-sell-online">How to Decide What to Sell When You Don't Make Anything →</a></p>

  <div class="chapter  reveal"><span class="bignum">03</span><div class="num">Chapter Three</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="page">The one product page that does the selling</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">You don't need a beautiful store. You need one product page that names the product in her words, gives her three reasons, tells her the truth about sizing and shipping before she scrolls, and answers the objection she's already thinking.</p>
  <p class="reveal">When a stranger clicks through from a group, she doesn't land on your homepage. She lands on one product, and that page has about eight seconds to do everything. Most new store pages waste them on a supplier description and a mockup floating on white.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>A title in her words.</strong> Not the clever name you came up with. The words she'd type to find it. "Night shift survival mug" beats "The Luna Mug" because she recognises herself in the first and has to decode the second.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Three benefits, not features.</strong> A feature is "11oz ceramic, dishwasher safe." A benefit is "big enough for the whole first coffee of a twelve-hour shift." Write three of those and cut the rest. Long descriptions are where doubt lives.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Sizing and shipping honesty above the fold.</strong> If it's apparel, the size chart goes near the top, with a plain line about whether it runs small. If it ships in five to eight days from a US warehouse, say so before she adds to cart, not in a policy page. Shipping surprises abandon more carts than anything else, and print on demand is made to order, so she can't send it back because it didn't fit. Tell her that kindly and early.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Mockups on real backgrounds.</strong> A mug on a kitchen counter in morning light. A tee on a hanger against a real wall. The product should look like it already lives in her life, not in a catalogue.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>An FAQ that answers the objection.</strong> She's thinking "will this fade in the wash," "what if the colour looks different," "how long until it arrives." Put those three on the page with straight answers. You already know them — they're the ones you asked before you ordered your own sample.</p>
  <div class="box tip reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✦</span> The Edit</span><p>Read your product page out loud as the buyer, phone in one hand, on a break. Every sentence that makes you skim gets cut. Every question that pops into your head gets answered on the page.</p></div>
  <p class="deeper reveal"><span>Go deeper —</span> <a href="/learn/shopify-store-in-a-weekend">How to Set Up a Shopify Store in a Weekend →</a></p>

  <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">04</span><div class="num">Chapter Four</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="twenty">A list of twenty people</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">Start an email list the day the store goes live, aim for twenty names before you aim for a hundred, and make the first email a story and a launch, not a newsletter.</p>
  <p class="reveal">The uncomfortable truth about the group strategy: it works, and you don't own any of it. The admin can close the group tomorrow. An email address is the only piece of that attention you get to keep.</p>
  <p class="reveal">So the store needs a way to collect one from day one. Shopify's built-in signup and a simple pop-up are enough. Offer something small in exchange: a sizing guide, a care card, a first-order discount if your margin allows it. Twenty people who asked to hear from you is a real list, and it's the one most stores never build.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>The first email is not a newsletter.</strong> Nobody signed up for "updates." Write the story of the product instead: the problem you noticed, the sample you ordered, what was wrong with the first version and what you changed. Then tell her it's live and give her the link. That's a launch. It reads like a person talking, because it is one.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Send the second one anyway.</strong> The first email might get two clicks and no orders. That's normal. The second, a week later, is where a small list starts to behave like a list. One product, one reason, one link.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Twenty subscribers reading two honest emails will get you closer to ten sales than a thousand followers who scrolled past a post. If you've never written a sales email, start with the five in the piece below.</p>
  <div class="box check reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✓</span> The first-email shape</span><p>The problem you noticed · The sample you ordered · What you fixed · It's live, here's the link. Four short paragraphs. No subject line tricks.</p></div>
  <p class="deeper reveal"><span>Go deeper —</span> <a href="/learn/email-marketing-for-beginners">How to Write Your First 5 Emails →</a></p>

  <div class="chapter  reveal"><span class="bignum">05</span><div class="num">Chapter Five</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="faceless">The faceless version of "sharing your store"</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">You share the store the same way any founder does, minus the selfie. A brand account, mockups that look real, product-in-use photos from the supplier, and a founder note without a photo. The brand's face is the product.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Every "first sales" piece eventually says <em>share it everywhere</em>, and pictures a woman holding her product up to a phone camera. If that's not you, fine. Most of the stores you've bought from never showed you a founder either. You know their logo and their product.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>A brand account, not a personal one.</strong> Name it after the store. Everything it posts is about the product and who it's for. Nobody expects a brand account to show a face, so nobody notices when it doesn't.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Mockups that don't look like mockups.</strong> Your print on demand app gives you basic ones. Use AI image tools to place the same design in a real-looking scene: the mug on a nurse's station, the tote on a café chair. Keep it honest — it must look like the product she'll receive.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Supplier photos of the product in use.</strong> Printful, Printify and the others often provide lifestyle shots of their blanks: the hoodie on a model, the tumbler on a desk. Those are yours to use for products you sell through them. Check each app's terms once.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>A founder note without a photo.</strong> Your About page needs a person behind it. Write one paragraph, first person, about why this product and who it's for. Sign it with a first name. It does the trust work a headshot would.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Faceless doesn't mean nobody's home. It means your face is never required. The product, the words and the care in the packaging are what she remembers.</p>
  <div class="box tip reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✦</span> The Edit</span><p>If you've planned the page and the list and still haven't opened the store, <a href="https://shopify.pxf.io/eK3xYz" rel="sponsored noopener" target="_blank">Shopify's $1-a-month start</a> (affiliate link) covers the first three months. Prices are Shopify's and can change.</p></div>
  <p class="deeper reveal"><span>Go deeper —</span> <a href="/learn/how-to-build-a-faceless-digital-business">How to Build a Faceless Digital Business →</a></p>

  <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">06</span><div class="num">Chapter Six</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="after-ten">What to change after sale ten</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">Read the ten orders like data, not like a celebration. Which product, which page, which source. Then double down on the one thing that worked and cut the rest.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Sale ten is a stopping point, not a finish line. The temptation is to add five more products and join six more groups. Do the opposite. Open the ten orders and Shopify's analytics and answer three questions.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Which product?</strong> If you listed six and eight orders were one of them, you have one product and five distractions. Put the winner front and centre and stop spending effort on the rest.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Which page?</strong> If one product page converts and another with the same traffic doesn't, the difference is on the page. Compare the titles, the photos, the shipping line. Copy what worked across.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Where did they come from?</strong> Shopify shows the source of each session. If seven of ten came from one group thread and none from your brand account, that's not a reason to post more. It's a reason to answer more questions in that group. Follow the orders, not your feelings about which channel should have worked.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Then do more of the one thing. One product, one channel, one page, until the next ten arrive faster than the first ten did. That is what a store looks like when it starts to work: not louder, just narrower. The rest of the build is in <strong>The Shopify Edit</strong> inside the membership, but you don't need it until the first ten have told you what they know.</p>
  <div class="box check reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✓</span> The sale-ten review</span><p>Which product sold · Which page converted · Where the buyers came from. One answer each. Double down on those three. Cut everything else for now.</p></div>
  <p class="deeper reveal"><span>Go deeper —</span> <a href="/learn/shopify-store-guide">How to Start a Shopify Store →</a></p>

